# zenavif NEON vs forced-scalar (YUV->RGB) — first real tier comparison
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cargo bench --bench tier_isolation --features _dev
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native

## Result

| size      | NEON     | scalar    | speedup |
|-----------|----------|-----------|---------|
| 1920x1080 |  9.24ms  |  22.36ms  |  2.42x  |
| 512x256   | 833.07us |  1274.5us | (1.53x, see caveat) |

Quote the 1080p number. The 512x256 NEON arm reports CV=38% — at ~800us the
measurement is noisy enough that 1.53x is not a number to rely on. The 1080p
rows are clean.

## Why this bench had to be added

`benches/yuv_conversion_benchmark.rs` is titled "SIMD vs scalar" but never runs
a scalar arm. It probes `Desktop64::summon()` — an x86 alias — and prints
whether AVX2 is present, then benchmarks one unlabelled configuration. On
aarch64 that probe ALWAYS fails, so the bench prints

  "AVX2/FMA not available - using scalar fallback"

while NEON is in fact running, and reports the NEON time as if it were scalar.
Anyone reading that output on ARM would conclude this crate has no working SIMD
and that its conversion is slow, when it is 2.4x faster than scalar.

That bench is left in place (it is a valid throughput bench); this one adds the
comparison it claims to make.

## Input note

The existing bench fills every plane with `vec![128u8; ..]` — a constant image.
This one uses varied planes: a branch-free kernel will not care much, but a
constant input exercises none of the value-dependent behaviour and makes the
result easier to over-trust.
